---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ContinuityDemo from '../components/demos/ContinuityDemo.astro';
import OrchestratorDemo from '../components/demos/OrchestratorDemo.astro';
import RecallDemo from '../components/demos/RecallDemo.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Use cases"
  description="Three interactive walk-throughs: continue work across machines, run many workers from one orchestrator, and ask your session history."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Use cases</span>
      <h1>Three things it is good at.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Each of these is a small simulation you can poke at. The data is invented and nothing leaves this page, but the
        endpoints and tool names are the real ones.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight" id="continuity">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="uc-head">
        <span class="uc-n">01</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Pick up where you left off</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Work rarely finishes on the machine it started on. Drover keeps the session history on the hub, so you can
            continue on another host, in another harness, and say yes to a prompt from wherever you are.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ContinuityDemo />
      <div class="uc-notes">
        <div>
          <h4>What is real</h4>
          <p>
            <code>POST /harness/sessions/&#123;id&#125;/continue</code> starts a new session on a target host and harness, briefed from the
            source session. Approvals are relayed for Claude Code sessions. The iOS app shows and answers them.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Good to know</h4>
          <p>
            A handoff reuses the same working directory path, so the project should live at the same path on both
            machines. Summaries and open loops need a summarizer backend; plain history does not.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight band" id="orchestrator">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="uc-head">
        <span class="uc-n">02</span>
        <div>
          <h2>One orchestrator, many workers</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            You already have something that plans work: OpenClaw, Hermes, or a script you wrote on a Sunday. Point it at
            Drover and it gets a fleet. It launches sessions on any host, sends turns, watches status, and keeps its own
            review and merge loop.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <OrchestratorDemo />
      <div class="uc-notes">
        <div>
          <h4>What is real</h4>
          <p>
            Session launch, turns, fleet status and publication records are all part of the authenticated{' '}
            <a href="/docs/api/">HTTP API</a>. Publication records note which branch and pull request a session produced.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Good to know</h4>
          <p>
            Drover does not open, review or merge pull requests. That judgement belongs to your orchestrator. Drover
            runs the workers and remembers what they did.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight" id="recall">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="uc-head">
        <span class="uc-n">03</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Ask your history</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            "Why did we drop that cache?" is a question with an answer, and the answer is in a session from two weeks ago.
            Any agent connected to the hub's MCP endpoint can ask. Type your own query or pick one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <RecallDemo />
      <div class="uc-notes">
        <div>
          <h4>What is real</h4>
          <p>
            <code>drover_search</code>, <code>drover_recall_bundle</code>, <code>drover_open_loops</code> and{' '}
            <code>drover_files_touched</code> are real <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP tools</a>. Results carry the answering store and a data
            watermark.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Good to know</h4>
          <p>
            The ranking in this demo is a toy keyword scorer in your browser. Real recall is keyword search by default,
            with semantic recall when you configure an embeddings backend.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section center">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <h2>Want to try it on real sessions?</h2>
      <div class="btn-row" style="justify-content:center;margin-top:1.4rem">
        <a class="btn" href="/docs/getting-started/">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/architecture/">How it works</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .uc-head {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.4rem;
    align-items: start;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
  }
  .uc-n {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    line-height: 0.9;
    color: transparent;
    -webkit-text-stroke: 1.5px var(--ember);
  }
  .uc-head h2 {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .uc-notes {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 320px), 1fr));
    gap: 1rem 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
  }
  .uc-notes h4 {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .uc-notes p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }
  .uc-notes code {
    white-space: normal;
  }
</style>
